1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A receiver sees a stream of bits. Nothing in the stream says where one message stops.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
A flag pattern marks the ends. The sender must guarantee that pattern never appears inside the data.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Bit stuffing: after five ones the sender inserts a zero, and the receiver removes it.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Destuffing is the same rule backwards: delete the zero after each run of five ones.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
One parity bit answers one question: is the number of ones even?

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Add a parity bit per row and per column and a single error is not just detected, it is located.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Four errors at the corners of a rectangle leave every row and every column even.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
So the honest statement: all one, two and three-bit errors, and some four-bit errors escape.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The Internet checksum adds the data as 16-bit words in one's complement.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
It is cheap: one adder, no division. That is why IP, TCP and UDP all use it.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Two errors that add to the same total cancel: one word up by one, another down by one.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
So: checksum for speed, CRC for strength. Neither is a correction code.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A CRC treats the bits as a polynomial over GF(2) and divides by a generator.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Append r zeros, divide, and send the message with the r-bit remainder attached.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
At the receiver the whole codeword must divide exactly: remainder zero means no detected error.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
What is caught: every burst of length at most r, and every odd-weight error if x+1 divides the generator.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Hamming distance is the number of positions in which two words differ.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
To detect s errors you need d min at least s+1. To correct t errors you need d min at least 2t+1.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Hamming code places check bits at the powers of two, so each position has a unique binary address.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The failed checks, read as a binary number, are the position of the bad bit.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
One shared channel, many stations, no coordinator: somebody has to lose.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Pure ALOHA: transmit at will. A frame is safe only if no other frame starts within one frame time of it.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Slotted ALOHA halves the window, because a frame may only start at a slot boundary.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Maximum throughput: 18.4 percent for pure, 36.8 percent for slotted, both at the sweet spot load.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Carrier sense: listen before you talk. Most collisions come from not having heard yet.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The window is one propagation delay, not two: only frames started within tau of yours hurt you.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Collision detection needs the sender still transmitting when the echo of the collision returns.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
So the minimum frame size is twice the one-way propagation delay times the rate: L at least 2 tau R.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
An 802.3 frame: destination, source, type or length, payload, and a four-byte CRC.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Sixty-four bytes minimum, because 512 bit times is the slot time that brackets a round trip.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
After a collision the station waits a random number of slots, doubling the window each time.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
A switch gives each port its own collision domain; a hub does not.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Write down tau, R, L and v before touching any formula.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Minimum frame size: L equals 2 tau R. Given any three, the fourth follows.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
The ratio a equals tau over transmission time. Small a means efficient.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Payload efficiency: 46 of 64 bytes at the minimum, 1500 of 1518 at the maximum.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: is the question about detecting, correcting, or sharing?

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: for detection, ask what the generator or the code guarantees, not what it hopes.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: for a sharing question, find tau, then decide whether detection is required.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Step four: convert back to the unit asked for, then check the magnitude.

